    "composition_parti": {
      "group": "massing-and-roof",
      "binding": "specified",
      "variants": [
        {
          "id": "additive-vernacular",
          "status": "forbidden",
          "note": "Every member of this family \u2014 the strict-symmetrical institutional stream (Egyptian Revival, Renaissance Revival, Octagon House) as much as the picturesque stream (Gothic Revival, Italianate) \u2014 is composed by an architect or pattern-book author toward a historical or theoretical argument, never grown by accretion the way a real vernacular building is. Downing's own theory (a house should be composed to be read in silhouette against the landscape) is explicitly a composition doctrine, not a growth doctrine, even where the result looks irregular."
        }
      ],
      "rule": "The family is designed, not accreted, in every one of its nine members \u2014 but the family splits close to evenly on WHAT kind of design discipline governs: Egyptian Revival, Renaissance Revival and Octagon House hold strict bilateral (or, for the octagon, radially regular) symmetry as a compositional law in its own right, while Gothic Revival and Italianate treat picturesque irregularity and silhouette hierarchy as the compositional law instead. Which of those two disciplines governs is a per-style decision the family does not adjudicate.",
      "confidence": "high",
      "note": "The family's own defining_characteristics list 'Irregular, asymmetrical massing' as if it were universal, but three of the nine members (Egyptian Revival, Renaissance Revival, Octagon House) explicitly forbid the massed-picturesque massing and hold strict-bilateral or radially-regular symmetry instead \u2014 Renaissance Revival calls picturesque irregularity the thing that 'destroys the only compositional rule the style has.' The family description is written from the Gothic/Italianate half of the family; this kit does not repeat that overclaim.",
      "status": "drafted"
    },
